        "Competition can be examined by:\n- Customer type\n- Price tier\n- Use case\n- Geography\n- Distribution channel\n- Design preference\n- Product size\n- Performance requirement\n- Buying context\n- Level of service\nThe transcript's earmuff example argues that a generic category may contain a gap for fashion-forward designs. The point is not that the opportunity is proven. It is that a founder should inspect what different customer groups actually want rather than treating every product in a broad category as equivalent.\nSegment the market\nFor each competing product, ask:\n- Who is it designed for?\n- Who is excluded or poorly served?\n- What does it do well?\n- What do customers complain about?\n- Is it too expensive, too cheap, too bulky, unattractive, fragile, or difficult to use?\n- Does it require a compromise that a specific customer segment dislikes?\n- Is the weakness important enough that people would pay to fix it?\nThe gap must matter\nA product can be objectively better and still fail if customers do not care enough to pay for the improvement.",
